@@s001 NP and NP-completeness
@@s002 ok:in the certificate (covers the edge),no:edge not covered,dato:node of the certificate
@@s003 Problem: Vertex Cover of size k = 2? The <b>certificate</b> is a list of 2 nodes: <b>b</b> and <b>d</b>. I show it in one line: it is polynomial to write (2 nodes out of 5).
@@s004 Verification, edge by edge: ab (b c e), bc (b c e), ad (d c e), bd, be (b), ce (neither of the two is in the certificate), de (d). Every edge touches b or d, <b>except ce</b>.
@@s005 Correct certificate: {b, e}. Every edge has an endpoint in the list: ab, bc, ad, bd, be, ce (e), de (e). The verification costs <b>O(n + m)</b>: it is the polynomial time that makes the problem in NP.
@@s006 NP and NP-completeness
@@s007 NP are the yes/no problems with a short certificate that can be verified in polynomial time. NP-complete means in NP and NP-hard. This page explains the two concepts and why the proof of NP-hardness is done with reductions.
@@s008 P and NP: what it means
@@s009 You work only with <b>yes/no</b> problems (the decision version). A class is a set of problems with the same difficulty.
@@s010 <b>P</b> = the problems that are <b>solved</b> in polynomial time, <code>O(n<sup>k</sup>)</code> with fixed k. Examples: shortest path, matching, 2-SAT.<br> <b>NP</b> = the problems in which a solution is <b>verified</b> in polynomial time, if someone hands it to you. Examples: Vertex Cover, 3-SAT, Hamiltonian cycle.
@@s011 The difference is <i>finding</i> versus <i>checking</i>. Sudoku: filling it in is difficult, checking an already filled sudoku is fast.
@@s012 Every problem in P is also in NP (if you can solve it, you can also verify it): <b>P ⊆ NP</b>. It is not known whether the converse holds too: it is the question <b>P = NP?</b>, still open.
@@s013 NP does not mean non-polynomial
@@s014 NP stands for <i>nondeterministic polynomial</i>, not for "not polynomial". Easy problems are inside NP.
@@s015 The certificate
@@s016 A problem is in <b>NP</b> if for every yes-instance there exists a <b>certificate</b> (a proof) of polynomial size, and a verifier checks it in polynomial time. Example: vertex cover of size k. Certificate: the k nodes. Verification: for each edge, one of the two endpoints is in the list. Time <code>O(n + m)</code>.
@@s017 Being in NP does not mean being difficult
@@s018 Avoid or Lockout are in P, hence in NP. The question "is it in NP?" only asks to find the certificate.
@@s019 The certificate is checked in polynomial time
@@s020 NP-complete and Cook-Levin
@@s021 <b>NP-hard</b>: at least as hard as every problem in NP. <b>NP-complete</b>: in NP and NP-hard. <b>Cook-Levin theorem</b>: Circuit-SAT is NP-complete. From there, through reductions, one obtains 3SAT, Independent Set, Vertex Cover, Hamiltonian cycle and many others.
@@s022 Assuming P ≠ NP: if an NP-complete problem were in P, then P = NP. In the proofs you do not discuss the case P = NP.
@@s023 At the exam
@@s024 At the exam
@@s025 "Show that the problem is in NP": (a) decision version, (b) certificate and its length, (c) verification in polynomial time. Phrase for the correctness of an algorithm: <i>"If the algorithm were correct, an NP-complete problem would be in P, so P = NP."</i>
